This is a note file explaning why we should separate declarations and definitions.

 Why We Split Declarations (.h) and Definitions (.c)
=====================================================

1. Sharing across files
   Multiple .c files can #include the same .h to use a function,
   without each needing (or being allowed) to redefine it. The
   header is the "contract"; only one .c file actually implements it.

2. Faster compilation
   Each .c file compiles independently into a .o file. If you only
   change the implementation in function.c, only that file needs
   recompiling -- not everything that uses it. Change the header
   (the interface), though, and everything that includes it does
   need a rebuild.

3. Encapsulation / hiding implementation details
   Other files only see what's in the header (the "what"), not the
   actual logic (the "how") in the .c file. This keeps internal
   details private and the public interface clean.

4. Avoiding "multiple definition" linker errors
   If you put full function definitions in a header and included
   that header in multiple .c files, you'd get duplicate symbol
   errors at link time. Declarations are safe to include everywhere;
   definitions are not.

In short: headers = "here's what exists and how to use it,"
source files = "here's how it actually works." It's a separation
between interface and implementation.

